<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Unique Facts About My Area – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Visiting Our Country</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Unique Facts About My Area</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Mediate information from notices or announcements for broader understanding. Concept: Interpret graphical data for classroom use.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain basic information from local area notices or events, using gestures and visuals (e.g., "The museum is open on weekends," while pointing to weekend days on a calendar). Can interpret visitor information charts for peers and explain the significance of highlighted points (e.g., "This map shows the museum and beach locations. Visiting the museum first makes sense because it's closer to the bus stop."). Can summarize key details of an announcement by repeating main ideas (e.g., "The event starts at 3 p.m." while pointing to the time on a digital clock display).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of various local signs, notices, and simple tourist maps (e.g., "Museum Hours," "Beach Safety Rules," a map showing "Cafeteria," "Restrooms," "Exit"). Students quickly identify what kind of information each visual provides. For example, "This sign gives directions to the beach." or "This notice talks about event times." The teacher prompts students with, "What information can you get from this?" and "Who would need this information?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher presents a simple, short announcement about a local historical tour (e.g., "Explore Casco Viejo! Daily tours start at 9 AM and 2 PM from Central Plaza. Learn about our unique history. Adults: $15, Children: $7."). The teacher then models how to mediate this information to a peer who doesn't understand English well. "They said the tour starts at 9 in the morning, and again at 2 in the afternoon. It's about our city's history. An adult ticket is fifteen dollars." The teacher explicitly uses reported speech and gestures (pointing to times, cost). The teacher highlights contrastive sounds in "tour" /tʊər/ and "start" /stɑːrt/, and pronounces "interesting" (/ˈɪn.trə.stɪŋ/) naturally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If my friend only speaks Spanish, what is my job when I see this English announcement?" (Expected: "You explain it to them in a way they understand.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I mediate information, do I just repeat the words, or do I make it clear and easy to understand?" (Expected: "You make it clear and easy, perhaps using simpler words or visuals.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Visiting Our Country".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input:</b> The teacher provides a simulated "Tourist Information Board" with several authentic-looking elements: a simple map of a local area (e.g., Casco Viejo) highlighting 3-4 landmarks (cathedral, museum, market), a short event announcement (e.g., "Night Market every Friday, 6 PM - 10 PM. Music & local food!"), and a visitor statistics chart (e.g., "Most Visited Attractions Last Year: Museum (40%), Beach (30%), Market (20%), Other (10%)").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Infographic / Tourist Information Board (map, short announcement, simple bar chart). Register: Informative, descriptive. Format: Visuals with concise text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret graphical data for classroom use. Explain basic information from local area notices or events, using gestures and visuals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students answer gist questions: "What is the purpose of this board?" (To help tourists plan their visit). "What kind of unique facts about our area can we find here?" (Info about history, local events, popular locations).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False: Students read statements and decide if they are true or false based on the board. E.g., "The Night Market is open on Saturdays." (False). "The museum is the most visited attraction." (True).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Information Matching: Match specific questions to the relevant section of the board. E.g., "Where can I find souvenirs?" (Market section). "When is the next local event?" (Announcement section).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice mediating information using Past simple/present perfect (e.g., "Casco Viejo was once a neglected area, but now it has become popular"), Future with "will"/"going to" for intentions (e.g., "You will find the market here," "We're going to see the cathedral"), Reported speech (e.g., "The notice said the event starts at 6 PM"), and key vocabulary (visit, area, location, attraction, plan, direction, market, museum, restaurant, beach, town, history, blocks, cathedral, accommodations, landmark, guide, itinerary, precaution, safety, transportation). Pronunciation will focus on /st/ in "stay," /ks/ in "explain," and reduced syllables in "restaurant." Pragmatic function: asking for recommendations, describing landmarks. Sociolinguistic: participating in group planning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Fill-in-the-Blanks:</b> Students receive a worksheet with short descriptions of local facts or directions, each with a gap. They must choose the correct vocabulary word (e.g., "Our city's _____ has many old buildings." - history) or grammar structure (e.g., "The guide ______ (say) we should turn right at the museum." - said) to complete the sentences. The sentences are based on the Tourist Information Board.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Guided Interpretation:</b> In pairs, students receive a specific section of the Tourist Information Board (e.g., only the map, or only the event announcement, or only the statistics chart). They must prepare 2-3 sentences to "mediate" or explain this information to another pair. For instance, explaining the map: "This map shows the museum and beach locations. Visiting the museum first makes sense because it's closer to the bus stop." or explaining the chart: "The chart shows that the museum has been a popular attraction for tourists, while the market has fewer visits. We're going to arrange a visit to the museum." The teacher circulates, providing feedback on language use, especially reported speech and future tenses, and pronunciation of specific sounds like in "restaurant" and "interesting."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - "Guide Your Partner":</b> Students work in new pairs. One student is a "Local Guide" and the other is a "Visitor." The Local Guide uses the Tourist Information Board to answer 3 questions from the Visitor. The Visitor asks questions like, "What unique places should I visit?" or "What's the best direction to the market?" The Guide must mediate the information from the board, using expressions like "The map shows you should go straight..." or "They said the market is active on Fridays." Students then switch roles.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Visiting Our Country".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative group work to prepare a segment of their "Directions Video Tutorial." Students will work in groups of 3-4. Each group chooses one specific local landmark or area discussed in previous lessons (e.g., Casco Viejo, Amador Causeway, a local market) and will plan how to present directions and unique facts about it for their video. They will specifically focus on how they will mediate information from various sources (notices, maps, local knowledge) into clear, concise directions and interesting facts for their video viewers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In their groups, students select one landmark for their video segment.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They brainstorm unique facts and important directions related to this landmark. They should use a simple drawn map or existing pictures/notices as their "source" material.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups then collaboratively draft a script for their 1-2 minute video segment. The script must include:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">An introduction to the landmark (e.g., "Today, we're going to explore the breathtaking Casco Viejo, a truly unique area!").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least three clear directions from a starting point (e.g., "From the Central Plaza, you will turn left at the cathedral and walk two blocks. The museum will be on your right."). They must use future tenses ("will" and "going to") and prepositions of place/direction.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A mention of a unique fact or historical detail, possibly using reported speech (e.g., "Local guides said that this beautiful church has a lot of history.") or past simple/present perfect (e.g., "This area was once neglected, but it has become a must-see attraction").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups practice mediating their information to each other, ensuring clarity and using gestures/visuals effectively, as they would for the video. The teacher observes and offers suggestions for improving clarity and linguistic accuracy, especially for pronouncing /tʃ/ in "chance" and /ʃ/ in "showcase."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their drafted video segment to the class. They act out their directions and explanation, using their simple maps or visuals to mediate the information. They focus on clearly explaining the unique facts and directions. The teacher uses an observation checklist to assess their mediation skills and use of target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Mediation:</b> Information from source (map/notice) is explained clearly and concisely to the "audience." (Learning Outcome: Can explain basic information; Can interpret graphical data).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Gestures/Visuals:</b> Effectively uses gestures and points to visuals to aid understanding.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Target Grammar:</b> Accurately uses future with "will" and "going to" for directions, reported speech, and past simple/present perfect.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Use:</b> Incorporates relevant vocabulary (e.g., landmark, direction, museum, history, unique).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Generally intelligible pronunciation, with attention to contrastive sounds and reduced syllables.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Participates effectively in group planning and presentation.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One thing I did well today to help explain information to others was...</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One specific grammar point or vocabulary word I used successfully in our video plan was...</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel now explaining directions or unique facts about my area to someone who is visiting?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of preparing our 'Directions Video Tutorial' did I find most challenging, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this mediation lesson? Students were engaged when interpreting the graphical data and planning their video segment. The role-play in Stage 3 really helped them practice explaining clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed? Some students struggled with consistently using reported speech correctly when mediating announcements. I should provide more sentence starters or specific examples for reported speech in a future review. Also, ensuring all groups had sufficient visual aids for their presentation would enhance their mediation practice.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to future application of skills:</b> The skills developed in mediating information, especially giving clear directions and explaining unique facts, are directly transferable to real-life situations where students might act as local guides or assist tourists. The practice with reported speech and future tenses will also be crucial for describing plans and recounting information in future communication tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>